      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:39:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we have a reason to not explicitly call out that this is designed for service users?\n\nAlso - if a deployment has a set of service users that are exempt from PCI rules, is the deployment still PCI compliant?",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_34625517",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:41:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Or - does PCI need to have a hard requirement on using x509 certificates for service users, eliminating the password problem all together?",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_f4fbad45",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_374c1776",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:43:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Probably not. However, I expect that operators will implement PCI differently; thus, have different config settings. Often, it\u0027s a matter of how you interpret the standards. Plus, PCI standards will change over time.",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_f4fbad45",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_b7ae47e5",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:44:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As far as I know, there isn\u0027t a hard requirement on x509 certs.",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_374c1776",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_57cefbb4",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:48:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regarding explicitly calling out service users, I think it could be used for some admins as well. I\u0027m open to suggestions though.",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_b7ae47e5",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_976ea394",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:50:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The other thing to consider is that even though we are adding this for PCI, an operator could be using it for meeting HIPAA standards.  Or, to simply meet their specific security requirements.",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_57cefbb4",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_b7e64714",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T18:52:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Implement PCI differently as in not rely on what we\u0027re introducing into Keystone for PCI requirements?\n\nI guess the thing I\u0027m thinking about is does it make sense to provide PCI with an ignore_list of the ignore_list violates PCI in some way. Does that make sense? Of course, I don\u0027t claim to be a PCI expert here, just something I thought of.",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_374c1776",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9abb7d3a_57eb9bb0",
        "filename": "keystone/common/config.py",
        "patchSetId": 45
      },
      "lineNbr": 1053,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-02T19:00:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As much as I\u0027d like to say, that if you have these config settings, you\u0027re PCI compliant, I don\u0027t think we can. It would be based on our interpretation of the standards. And it would be very difficult for us to maintain that as the standards change over time. Ultimately, I think we want to provide operators security features to configure and let them decide whether or not they are PCI compliant.",
      "parentUuid": "9abb7d3a_b7e64714",
      "revId": "f2594d571b5e7d7dbe08aef5dbe07c0112adc352",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    }
